issue.body:
In my test suite, I have banners that look like

``` JS
['original', 'minified'].forEach(function (module) {
  var mod = require(module);
  //////////////////////////
  /**/  suite("Foo, ")  /**/
  //////////////////////////
});
```

If they are mis-indented, the second line of the banner is gradually eaten.

``` JS
[...].forEach(function (module) {
  var mod = require(module);
  // please mind the margin
    //////////////////////////
    /**/  suite("Foo, ")  /**/
    //////////////////////////
});
```

After one run: 

``` JS
    //////////////////////////
    /**/suite("Foo, ")  /**/
    //////////////////////////
```

After two runs:

``` JS
    //////////////////////////
    /*suite("Foo, ")  /**/
    //////////////////////////
```

Then it stays like this. I guess it's full.

---

Edit: more info, thanks eslintbot.

Version: v1.10.3 and v2.0.0-alpha-1
Config: 

``` JS
module.exports = {
    rules: {
        indent: [ // commenting out this rule 
            2, 2  // makes the problem
        ],        // go away
        quotes: [
            2,
            "single",
            "avoid-escape"
        ],
        "linebreak-style": [
            2,
            "unix"
        ],
        semi: [
            2,
            "always"
        ],
        "no-trailing-spaces": [2]
    },
    parserOptions: { // for v2.0
        ecmaVersion: 6,
        sourceType: "module"
    }, 
    ecmaFeatures: { // for v1.x
        modules: true
    },
    env: {
        es6: true,
        node: true,
        browser: true
    },
    extends: "eslint:recommended"
};
```

ESLint is run from a NPM script as `eslint --fix foo.js`. Installed as a local dependency.

---

Edit2: It also happens with v2.0 alpha-1

---

Edit3 It doesn't happen at the top level, only in blocks (at least functions). I've updated the example.
